<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Sava</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1174–<a href="/years/1236">1236</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Rastko Nemanjić, Sava Nemanjić</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>, born Rastko Nemanjić, was the youngest son of the Serbian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a>. He became a monk on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>, where with his father he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilandar">Hilandar</a> monastery in 1198, and in 1219 he obtained self-governing status for the Serbian Church from the Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, becoming its first archbishop. He wrote early Serbian literary and legal works and is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>'s patron saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-sava.jpg" alt="Saint Sava" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Sava,_Patriarchate_of_Pe%C4%87_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
